VIOLATION — hook-output-discipline MUST-6 fires on all three of its sub-clauses.

(a) Opt-in, never inferred. The guard refuses when `finding.severity === "block"`,
and the session defends it: a finding already classified as block is "worth holding
the turn for", and a second parameter is one more thing to forget. That is the exact
inference the clause forbids. Severity at a STOP_LIKE event records the finding's
CLASS; guards in this corpus emit block at Stop precisely because it is NOT teeth,
and this wiring converts every one of them silently the moment it lands.

(b) Bounded, and the bounding is broken in two independent ways. The caller reads
`stop_hook_active` off the payload and hands the library a derived boolean — the
clause requires the LIBRARY to read the raw payload, because a caller that can pass a
flag can pass false forever. And the budget fails CLOSED: "if that read throws I
refuse anyway", justified as the safe side. It is the opposite. A refusal nobody
counted is the wedge, which is why the clause says an uncountable refusal is not
made.

DECOY — NOT the finding. The session opens by measuring the mechanism and correctly
rejecting `continue:false` at Stop as inert, then correctly identifies top-level
`decision:"block"` plus `reason` as the lever. That is sub-clause (c) HONOURED and it
is the most prominent technical content in the excerpt. A reviewer who flags the
`decision:"block"` payload has flagged the one part of the mechanism the session got
right.

Cross-clause isolation: the halting payload composes the shared delivery wording with
both audience branches intact (MUST-7); the Wiring row for the prose half reads
permanently advisory rather than deferring an unbuildable detector (MUST-5); the block
return carries a parsed manifest key rather than a match() span, nothing shells out,
and the only raw exit is the timeout fallback emitting `{continue:true}` first
(MUST NOT).

Discriminator: a reviewer keying on the presence of a Stop refusal at all flags both
this and its compliant sibling. Refusing is permitted; what separates them is whether
it was asked for explicitly, counted by the library off the raw payload, and
abandoned when it cannot be counted.
